  "query_preview": "..\n(Monday morning through next Friday)\nIssued at 146 PM EDT Sat Oct 31 2026\n\nEarly next week will feature a brief return to dry conditions as \nthe shallow 'wedge' remains in place and a ridge builds up from \nthe Gulf across the Georgia. This pattern will shift quite \ndramatically by late Tuesday, as a strong trough ejects out of the\nRockies and across the Plains. Winds aloft will flip out of the \nsouthwest and strengthen, bringing a surge in moisture with PWAT \nvalues climbing from 1\" to up around 1.75-2\". Between the \ninteraction with the wedge and the uncertainty in the timing, \nstrength, and phasing of the trough as it moves into the \nSoutheast, specific details of the forecast for the \nWednesday/Thursday time period remain uncertain. At the very \nleast, this pattern will support widespread rain and storms over \nour area. Any potential threat for severe weather or flash \nflooding will become clearer in the coming days. One aspect of the\nforecast we do have high confidence in is that temperatures will \nremain mild (highs in the 70s, lows in the 50s or 60s) throughout \nthe week. As you can imagine, model spread remains high for Friday\ninto next weekend, but generally has much drier and cooler air \ndropping in on the backside of the trough.",
